=== The Doctrine and Its Grounds

#smallcaps[Having] opened the words in the previous chapters as far as is necessary to uncover the foundation of the truth to be dwelt on and applied, I will lay it down in the following observation:

#emph[It is the great duty of all believers to use all diligence, in the ways Christ has appointed, so that they do not fall into temptation.]

I know that God knows how “to rescue the godly from temptations.” I know that he is “faithful,” that he “will not let us be tempted beyond what we can bear,” but “will also provide an escape.” Yet I dare say that I will convince all who pay attention to what is set down and written here that it is our great duty and concern to use all diligence, watchfulness, and care so that we do not enter into temptation. I will show it by the following considerations:

1\. In the #emph[brief summary] our Savior gave us of what we ought to pray for, this matter of not entering into temptation is expressly one heading. Our Savior knew how much it concerned us not to “enter into temptation” when he gave us this as one special subject of our daily dealings with God (Matt 6:13). And the order of the words shows us how important it is: “lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil.” If we are led into temptation, evil will befall us, more or less. How God may be said to tempt us, or to “lead us into temptation,” I showed before. In this direction what is meant is not so much his not #emph[giving us up to it] as his powerfully #emph[keeping us from it]. The last words are, as it were, an explanation or exposition of the former: “Lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from evil”; that is, “Deal with us in such a way that we may be powerfully delivered from the evil that comes with our entering into temptation.” Our blessed Savior knows our state and condition full well. He knows the power of temptations, having had experience of it (Heb 2:18). He knows our vain confidence, and the reserves of ability we think we have for dealing with temptations, as he found in Peter. But he knows our weakness and folly, and how soon we are thrown to the ground. That is why he made this provision of instruction at the very beginning of his ministry, to make us careful, if possible, in something of such great concern to us. If, then, we will place any confidence in the wisdom, love, and care of Jesus Christ toward us, we must grant the truth I am pleading for.

2\. Christ promises this freedom and deliverance as a great #emph[reward] of most acceptable obedience (Rev 3:10). This is the great promise made to the church of Philadelphia, in which Christ found nothing he would blame: “I will also keep you from the hour of temptation.” Not “You will be preserved #emph[in] it”; he goes higher: “You will be kept #emph[from] it.” Our Savior says: “There is an hour of temptation coming, a season that will make havoc in the world. Multitudes will then fall from the faith, and deny and blaspheme me. Oh, how few will be able to stand and hold out! Some will be utterly destroyed and perish forever. Some will receive wounds in their souls that will never be well healed while they live in this world; they will have their bones broken and go limping all their days. But,” he says, “ ‘because you have kept the word of My patience,’ I will be tender toward you, and ‘keep you from the hour of temptation.’ ” Surely what Christ promises in this way to his beloved church, as a reward of her service, love, and obedience, is no light thing. Whatever Christ promises to his bride is a fruit of unspeakable love; and that is so in a special way with what is promised as a reward of special obedience.

3\. To this end let us consider the general outcomes of men’s entering into temptation, both of bad men and of good: of unfounded professors, and of the choicest saints.

(1.) For the first, I will offer only one or two texts of Scripture. “The seeds on rocky ground are those who hear the word and receive it with joy, but they have no root. They believe for a season” (Luke 8:13). Well! How long do they believe? They are moved by the preaching of the word, and believe because of it; they make a profession and bring forth some fruits. But until when do they last? He says, “in the time of temptation, they fall away.” Once they enter into temptation they are gone forever. Temptation withers their whole profession and kills their souls. We see this fulfilled every day. Men have sat under the preaching of the gospel, been moved and delighted by it, and perhaps been regarded as believers, and have continued like this for some years. Yet no sooner does a temptation befall them that has vigor and staying power in it than they are turned out of the way and gone forever. They come to hate the word they delighted in, they despise those who profess it, and they are hardened by sin. So in Matt 7:26: “everyone who hears these words of Mine and does not act on them is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.” But what does this house of profession do for him? It shelters him, keeps him warm, and stands for a while. But, he says in verse 27, “The rain fell,” that is, temptation came, “and it fell—and great was its collapse!” Judas follows our Savior for three years, and all goes well with him. No sooner does he enter into temptation, and Satan gets hold of him and sifts him, than he is gone. Demas will preach the gospel until the love of the world befalls him, and then he is utterly turned aside. There would be no end of giving instances of this. With this sort of men, entering into temptation is entering into apostasy, more or less, in part or in whole; it never fails.

(2.) As for the #emph[saints] of God themselves, let us see by some instances what outcome their entering into temptation has had. I will name a few.

Adam was “the son of God” (Luke 3:38), created in the image of God, full of that integrity, righteousness, and holiness which could be, and was, an outstanding likeness of the holiness of God. He had a far greater store of ability in himself than we have, and he had nothing in him to entice or seduce him. Yet no sooner does this Adam enter into temptation than he is gone, lost, and ruined, he and all his descendants with him. What can we expect in the same condition, we who have in our temptations not only a #emph[cunning devil] to deal with, as he had, but a #emph[cursed world] and a #emph[corrupt heart] as well?

Abraham was the father of the faithful, whose faith is set forth as a pattern to all who will believe. Yet he entered twice into the same temptation, namely fear concerning his wife, and was twice overpowered by it, to the dishonor of God and, no doubt, to the disquiet of his own soul (Gen 12:12, 13; 20:2).

David is called “a man after My own heart” by God himself. Yet what a dreadful thing is the story of his entering into temptation! No sooner is he entangled than he is plunged into adultery. Then, seeking deliverance by his own devising, like a poor creature in a net, he is entangled more and more, until he lies like a dead man under the power of sin and folly.

I might mention Noah, Lot, Hezekiah, Peter, and the rest, whose temptations, and their falls in them, are on record for our instruction. Surely the man who has any heart in these things must say, as the people of Samaria said at Jehu’s letter, “ ‘If two kings could not stand against him, how can we?’ O Lord, if such mighty pillars have been thrown to the ground, and such cedars blown down, how shall I stand before temptations? Oh, keep me from entering in!” “Vestigia terrent” \[The footprints frighten me\]. Look at the footprints of those who have gone in. Whom do you see coming back without a wound, or at least a blemish? On this account the apostle would have us show tenderness toward those who have fallen into sin: “But consider yourself, or you also may be tempted” (Gal 6:1). He does not say, “or you also may sin, or fall, or be caught in a fault,” but, “or you also may be tempted.” That is, “You see the power of temptation in others, and you do not know how soon you may be tempted, nor what the state and condition of your soul will be then.” Certainly a man who has seen so many better and stronger men than himself fail and be thrown down in the trial will think it his duty to remember the battle and, if possible, to come there no more. Is it not madness for a man who is so weak he can hardly crawl about (and that is the case with most of us) not to avoid what he has seen giants defeated in attempting? You are still whole and sound. Beware of temptation, lest it go with you as it went with Abraham, David, Lot, Peter, Hezekiah, and the Galatians, who fell in the time of trial.

In nothing does the folly of men’s hearts show itself more openly in the days in which we live than in this cursed boldness of theirs in running into temptations and putting themselves in their way, after so many warnings from God and so many sad experiences every day before their eyes. They are ready for any society, any company, any conditions of outward advantage, without once weighing what their strength is, or what the concern of their poor souls is. Though they walk over the dead and the slain who fell before them just now in those very ways and paths, yet they will go on without heeding it or trembling. Through this door hundreds, thousands of professors have gone out within a few years. But:

4\. Let us #emph[consider ourselves], what our weakness is; and what temptation is, its power and efficacy, and what it leads to.

(1.) As for ourselves, we are #emph[weakness] itself. We have no strength, no power to withstand. Confidence in any strength in us is one great part of our weakness; it was so in Peter. The man who says he can do anything can do nothing as he should. And what is worse, it is the worst kind of weakness that is in us, a weakness from treachery, a weakness arising from the party that every temptation has within us. However strong and well fortified a castle or fort may be, if there is a treacherous party within, ready to betray it at every opportunity, there is no keeping it from the enemy. There are traitors in our hearts, ready to take the part of every temptation, to join with it and side with it, and to give up everything to it; yes, to solicit and bribe temptations to do the work, as traitors incite an enemy. Do not flatter yourselves that you#super[+] will hold out. There are secret lusts lurking in your#super[+] hearts, which perhaps do not stir now, but which, as soon as any temptation befalls you#super[+], will rise, make a tumult, cry out, disquiet, seduce, and never give up until they are either killed or satisfied. The man who promises himself that the frame of his heart will be the same under a temptation as it is before it will be woefully mistaken. “How could your servant, a mere dog, do such a monstrous thing?” says Hazael. Yes, you will be just such a dog if ever you become king of Syria; temptation arising from your interest will rob you of your humanity. The man who now abhors the very thought of this or that thing, if he once enters into temptation, will find his heart inflamed toward it, and all reasonings against it overpowered and silenced. He will laugh at his former fears, throw out his scruples, and despise the consideration he lived by. Little did Peter think that he would deny his Master and swear he did not know him as soon as he was ever asked whether he knew him or not. It went no better when the hour of temptation came. All his resolutions were forgotten, all his love for Christ buried; the present temptation, joining with his fleshly fear, carried everything before it.

To treat this a little more distinctly, I will consider what means of safety from the power of temptation, if we enter into it, may be expected from ourselves. I will consider them in general, as to their spring and source, and in particular, as to the ways of putting forth the strength we have, or seem to have.

\[1.\] In general, all we can look for is from our #emph[hearts]. As a man’s heart is, so is he. But what is the heart of a man in such a season?

1st. Suppose a man is not a #emph[believer], but only a #emph[professor] of the gospel. What can the heart of such a man do? “The heart of the wicked has little worth” (Prov 10:20); and surely what is of little worth in anything is not worth much in this. A wicked man may be of great use in outward things; but come to his heart, and it is false and a thing of nothing. Now, withstanding temptation is heart-work. When temptation comes like a flood, can such a rotten trifle as a wicked man’s heart stand before it? But I have spoken of these before. With them, entering into temptation and apostasy are one and the same.

2dly. Let it be whose heart it may, “He who trusts in his own heart is a fool” (Prov 28:26). The man who does so, be he who he may, is foolish in that. Peter did so in his temptation; he trusted in his own heart: “Even if all fall away on account of You, I never will.” It was his folly. But why was it his folly? Because he will not be delivered by it; his heart will not preserve him in snares or deliver him in temptations. A man’s heart will make him very fair promises before a temptation comes. “How could your servant, a mere dog, do such a monstrous thing?” says Hazael. “Even if all should deny You,” \[says Peter,\] “I will not. Shall I do this evil? It cannot be.” All the arguments fitted to check the heart in such a condition are mustered. Do you not think Peter did so? “What! Deny my Master, the Son of God, my Redeemer, who loves me? Can such ingratitude, unbelief, and rebellion befall me? I will not do it.” Shall a man, then, rest on his heart’s being steadfast? Let the wise man answer: “He who trusts in his own heart is a fool.” “The heart is deceitful” (Jer 17:9). We would not willingly trust anything in which there is any deceit or guile; and here is something that is “deceitful above all things.” It has a thousand tricks and treacheries to deal in. When it comes to the trial, every temptation will steal it away (Hos 4:11). Generally men’s hearts deceive them no more often than they trust in them; and then they never fail to do so.

\[2.\] Consider the particular ways and means that such a heart has, or can use, to safeguard itself in the hour of temptation, and their insufficiency for that purpose will quickly appear. I will give only a few instances.

1st. Love of #emph[honor] in the world. Reputation and esteem in the church, gained by earlier profession and walk, is one of the heart’s own weapons for defending itself in the hour of temptation. “Shall a man like me flee? I who have had such a reputation in the church of God, shall I lose it now by giving way to this lust, to this temptation, or by joining with this or that public evil?” This consideration has such an influence on the spirits of some that they think it will be a shield and buckler against any assaults that may befall them. They would die a thousand times before they would forfeit the reputation they have in the church of God! But, alas! this is only a green willow branch, or a new rope, to bind a giant of a temptation with. What do you think of “a third of the stars” (Rev 12:4)? Had they not shone in the sky of the church? Were they not aware, more than enough, of their own honor, height, usefulness, and reputation? But when the dragon comes with his temptations, he throws them down to the earth. Indeed, great temptations will make men who have no better defense fortify themselves, without their noticing it, against the dishonor and disrepute that come with their ways. “Populus sibilet, at mihi plaudo” \[Let the people hiss; I applaud myself\]. Do we not know instances still living of some who, after the glory of a long and useful profession, ventured on going along with wicked men? Within a while they found themselves cast down from their reputation with the saints because of it, and they hardened themselves against it and ended in apostasy (as in John 15:6). This did not keep Judas; it did not keep Hymenaeus or Philetus; it did not keep the stars of heaven; nor will it keep you.

2dly. There is, on the other side, the consideration of #emph[shame], reproach, loss, and the like. Men may also put their trust in this as a defense against temptations, and not doubt that they will be safeguarded and preserved by it. They would not for the world bring on themselves the shame and reproach that come with this or that failure! Now, this consideration extends only to #emph[open sins], such as the world takes notice of and abhors. So it is of no use at all in cases where pretexts and disguises may be invented and used; nor in public temptations to loose and careless walking, like those of our days; nor in cases that may be disputable in themselves, though plainly sinful to the consciences of those under temptation; nor in sins of the heart. In all these cases, and most other cases of temptation, countless reliefs are ready to be offered to the heart against this consideration. But besides all this, I say, we see by experience how easily this rope is broken once the heart begins to be entangled. Every corner of the land is full of examples of this.

3dly. They still have something that outweighs these lesser considerations: namely, that they will not wound their own #emph[consciences], disturb their peace, and bring themselves into danger of hellfire. Surely this, if anything, will preserve men in the hour of temptation. They will not squander their peace, or venture their souls by rushing headlong at God and the thick bosses of his shield! What could have more efficacy and power? I confess this is of great importance; and oh that it were weighed more than it is! Oh that we laid more weight on preserving our peace with God than we do! Yet I say that even this consideration will not preserve a man who is off his watch elsewhere and does not make it his work to follow the other rules dwelt on here. For:

(1st.) The peace of such a man may be #emph[false peace] or security, made up of presumption and false hopes; yes, even if he is a believer, it may be so. Such was David’s peace after his sin, before Nathan came to him. Such was the peace of Laodicea when it was ready to perish, and the peace of Sardis when it was dying. What should assure a soul that its peace is otherwise, since it is supposed not to labor universally to keep the word of Christ’s patience and to be watchful in all things? Do you think the peace of many in these days will be found to be true peace at last? Nothing of the kind. They go down alive to hell, and death will rule over them in the morning. Now, if a man’s peace is like this, do you think that something which cannot preserve itself can preserve him? It will give way at the first vigorous assault of a temptation in its height and hour. Like a broken reed, it will pierce the hand of the man who leans on it. But:

(2dly.) Suppose the #emph[peace] that is cared for, and set up to safeguard the soul, is true and good. Yet when everything is staked on this one venture, so many reliefs will be offered against this consideration when the hour of temptation comes that it will be made useless. “This evil is #emph[small]\; it is #emph[questionable]\; it does not fall openly and directly on the #emph[conscience]. I only fear the #emph[consequences]\; perhaps I can keep my peace in spite of it. Others of the people of God have fallen, and yet kept their peace or recovered it. If it is lost for a season, it may be gained again. I will not trouble its standing any more; or even if peace is lost, safety may remain.” There are a thousand pleas like these, all set up as batteries against this fort, so that it cannot hold out long.

(3dly.) Fixing on this one particular only is guarding one #emph[passage] or entrance while the enemy assaults us all around. It is true that a little armor would be enough to defend a man if he could choose where his enemy should strike him. But we are commanded to take “the full armor of God” if we mean to resist and stand (Eph 6). What we are speaking of is only one piece. When our eye is only on that, temptation may enter and prevail in twenty other ways. For instance, a man may be tempted to worldliness, unjust gain, revenge, vainglory, or the like. He fortifies himself with this consideration alone, that he will not do this thing, wound his conscience, and lose his peace. He fixes his eye on this particular, and counts himself safe as long as he is not overcome on that side. Meanwhile, perhaps, neglect of private communion with God, sensuality, and the like creep in, and he is not one bit better off than if he had fallen under the power of the part of the temptation that pressed on him most visibly. Experience shows that this consideration also does and will #emph[fail]. There is no saint of God who does not set a value on the peace he has; yet how many of them fail in the day of temptation!

4thly. But they have yet another consideration as well, and that is the vileness of sinning against God. How shall they do this thing and sin against God, the God of their mercies, of their salvation? How shall they wound Jesus Christ, who died for them? Surely this must preserve them. I answer:

First, we see this consideration failing too, every day. There is no child of God overcome by temptation who has not first overcome this consideration. So it is not a sure and unfailing defense.

Secondly, this consideration is of two kinds. Either it expresses the thoughts of the soul with particular reference to the temptation it is struggling with, and then it will not preserve the soul. Or it expresses the universal, habitual frame of heart that is in us on every account, and then it coincides with what I will offer later in this discourse as the universal medicine and remedy in this case. Of that I will speak later.

(2.) Consider #emph[the power of temptation], partly from what was shown before, from its #emph[effects] and fruits in the saints of old, and partly from other effects that we find ascribed to it in general. For instance:

\[1.\] It will #emph[darken the mind], so that a man will not be able to make a right judgment of things as he did before he entered into it. In the men of the world, the god of this world blinds their minds so that they do not see the glory of Christ in the gospel (2 Cor 4:4), and “promiscuity, wine, and new wine take away the heart” (Hos 4:11). So it is in the nature of every temptation, more or less, to take away the heart, or to darken the understanding of the person tempted.

And it does this in various ways:

1st. By #emph[fixing] the imagination and the thoughts on the #emph[object] it tends toward, so that the mind is turned aside from considering the things that would relieve and help it in the state it is in. A man is tempted to think that he is forsaken by God, that he is an object of God’s hatred, that he has no share in Christ. By the craft of Satan his mind will be so #emph[fixed] on considering this state and condition, with its distress, that he will not be able to make use of any of the reliefs suggested and offered to him against it. Following the fullness of his own thoughts, he will walk on in darkness and have no light. I say, a temptation will so possess the mind and fill it with thoughts of itself and its matter that it will draw the mind away from the clear consideration of things it otherwise could and would have. And the things the mind used to have a vigorous sense of, to keep it from sin, will by this means come to have no force or efficacy with it. Indeed, temptation will commonly bring men to such a state and condition that when others who know their state speak to them of the things that concern their deliverance and peace, their minds will be so possessed with the matter of their temptation that they will not understand at all, and will hardly hear one word that is said to them.

2dly. By #emph[woefully entangling the affections]. When the affections are engaged, everyone knows what influence they have in blinding the mind and darkening the understanding. If anyone does not know it, let him only open his eyes in these days, and he will quickly learn it. I will not now declare by what ways and means engaged affections cloud and darken the mind. I only say this: give me a man whose hope, love, or fear is engaged in any particulars where it ought not to be, and I will quickly show you where he is darkened and blinded. This, then, is where you will fail if you enter into temptation. The judgment you now have of things will not be utterly altered, but it will be darkened and made too weak to sway the will and master the affections. And the affections, set at liberty by temptation, will run on in madness. At once detestation of sin, abhorrence of it, the terrors of the Lord, the sense of love, and the presence of Christ crucified all depart, and leave the heart a prey to its enemy.

3dly. Temptation will give #emph[oil and fuel] to our lusts. It will incite and provoke them, and make them rage in a tumult beyond measure. It offers to a lust, a corruption, a suitable object, advantage, or occasion, and so it heightens and inflames it, and makes it wholly dominant for a season. So it dealt with fleshly fear in Peter, with pride in Hezekiah, with covetousness in Achan, with uncleanness in David, with worldliness in Demas, with ambition in Diotrephes. It will throw the reins on the neck of a lust and put spurs to its sides, so that it may rush forward like a horse into battle. A man does not know the pride, fury, and madness of a corruption until it meets with a suitable temptation. And now what will a poor soul think to do? His mind is darkened, his affections entangled, his lusts inflamed and provoked, his relief defeated. What will be the outcome of such a condition?

(3.) Consider that temptations are either #emph[public or private]\; and let us look a little at the efficacy and power of each separately.

\[1.\] There are public temptations, such as the one mentioned in Rev 3:10, which was to come upon the world “to test those who dwell on the earth”; or a combination of persecution and seduction for the trial of a careless generation of professors. Now, concerning such a temptation, consider:

1st. It has #emph[efficacy] with respect to God, who sends it to avenge the neglect and contempt of the gospel on the one hand, and the treachery of false professors on the other. So it will certainly accomplish what it receives a commission from him to do. When Satan offered his service to go out and seduce Ahab so that he might fall, God said to him, “You will surely entice him and prevail. Go and do it” (1 Kgs 22:22). He is permitted as to his wickedness, and commissioned as to the outcome and the punishment intended. When the Christian world was to be given up to folly and false worship for its neglect of the truth, and for its naked, barren, fruitless, Christ-dishonoring profession, it is said of the temptation that fell on them that “God will send them a powerful delusion so that they believe the lie” (2 Thess 2:11). What comes from God in this way, in a #emph[judicial] manner, has power with it and will prevail. In these days a selfish, spiritually slothful, careless, and worldly frame of spirit has infected almost the whole body of professors. If it has a commission from God to kill hypocrites, and to wound negligent saints, break their bones, and make them a scandal so that they may be ashamed, will it not have power and efficacy to do so? What havoc the spirit of error has made among us! Does it not come from this: that as some men did not delight to keep God in their hearts, so “He gave them up to a depraved mind” (Rom 1:28)? A man would think it strange, indeed it is a matter of amazement, to see persons of a sober spirit, who laid claim to great things in the ways of God, overcome, taken captive, ensnared, and destroyed by weak means, by stupid opinions and foolish imaginations, such as a man would think could never take hold of sensible or rational men, much less of professors of the gospel. But let us not think weak what God will have to be strong. No strength but the strength of God can stand in the way of the weakest things in the world that are commissioned by God for any end or purpose whatever.

2dly. In such temptations there is the secret influence of #emph[examples] in those who are counted #emph[godly] and are professors: “Because of the multiplication of wickedness, the love of most will grow cold” (Matt 24:12). The abounding of wickedness in some will, without their noticing it, throw water on the zeal and love of others, so that little by little it grows cold. Some begin to grow negligent, careless, worldly, and wanton. They break the ice toward pleasing the flesh. At first others blame them, judge them, perhaps rebuke them. In a short time their love too grows cold; and once the first shock is over, they too conform to them and are cast into the same mold with them. “A little leaven works through the whole batch of dough.” Paul repeats this saying twice (1 Cor 5:6; Gal 5:9). He wants us to take notice of it, and what he is speaking of is the danger that the whole body will be infected by the bad examples of some. We know how imperceptibly leaven goes on to give its flavor to the whole. So it is called a “root of bitterness” that “springs up to cause trouble and defile many” (Heb 12:15). If one little piece of leaven, if one bitter root, may endanger the whole, how much more when there are many roots of that kind, and much leaven is scattered abroad! It is easy to follow a multitude to do evil, and to say “A conspiracy” to all to whom the people say “A conspiracy.” Would anyone have thought it possible that this or that professor in our days would fall into ways of self, of the flesh, of the world? That they would play at cards and dice, revel and dance? That they would neglect family and private duties? That they would be proud, haughty, ambitious, worldly, covetous, oppressive? Or that they would be turned aside after foolish, empty, ridiculous opinions, deserting the gospel of Christ? In these two things lies the great temptation that has come upon us, the inhabitants of this world, to test us. But does not every man see that this has come about? And may we not see how it came about? Some loose, empty professors, who never had more than a form of godliness, began the way to these things when that form had served their turn. Then others began to go along with them a little, and to please the flesh in doing so. Little by little this has reached even the top boughs and branches of our profession, until almost all flesh has corrupted its way. And the man who departs from these iniquities makes his name a prey, if not his person.

3dly. Public temptations are usually accompanied by #emph[strong reasons and pretexts] that are too hard for men, or at least imperceptibly win them over to think less of the evil the temptation leads to. These give strength to the compound temptation that in these days has thrown the people of God down from their excellence, has cut their locks, and made them like other men. How full the world is of plausible pretexts and pleadings! There is the liberty and freedom of Christians, delivered from a frame of bondage. In my own observation I have seen a number of people going out through this door into sensuality and apostasy. They begin with light conduct, go on to neglect of the Sabbath and of public and private duties, and end in dissoluteness and profaneness. Then there is leaving public things to Providence and being content with things as they are. These things are good in themselves, but they have been argued into wretched, fleshly compromises, and into the utter ruin of all zeal for God and for the interest of Christ or his people in the world. These and similar considerations, joined with the ease and plenty, the greatness and promotion of professors, have brought things to this point: whereas by Providence we have changed places with the men of the world, by sin we have changed spirits with them too. We are like a plantation of men carried into a foreign country. In a short time they fall away from the manners of the people they came from and take up those of the country they have been brought into, as if there were something in the soil and the air that transformed them. Allow me to follow my comparison a little further. Look at the prevailing party of these nations, many of those in rule, power, and favor, with all their followers. Remember that they were a colony of Puritans, whose dwelling was “in a low place,” as the prophet speaks of the city of God, and that they have been moved by a high hand to the mountains they now possess. Whoever sees this cannot help wondering how soon they have forgotten the customs, manners, and ways of their own old people, and have been cast into the mold of those who went before them in the places they have been moved to. I speak of us all, especially of us who are among the lowest of the people, where perhaps this iniquity abounds most. What were those before us that we are not? What did they do that we do not? Prosperity has slain the foolish and wounded the wise.

\[2.\] Suppose the temptation is private. This has been spoken of before; I will add two things:

1st. Its #emph[union and incorporation] with lust, by which it gets within the soul and lies at the bottom of all it does. John tells us (1 John 2:16) that the things “in the world” are “the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life.” Now, it is evident that all these things are chiefly in the #emph[subject], not in the #emph[object]\; in the #emph[heart], not in the #emph[world]. But they are said to be “in the world” because the world gets into them, mixes itself with them, and unites and incorporates with them. As faith and the promises are said to be “mixed” (Heb 4:2), so lust and temptation are mixed. They twine together. They help each other on, and each of them grows higher and higher by the strength they give one another. Now, by this means temptation gets so deep into the heart that no reasonings against it can reach it. Nothing but what can kill the lust can conquer the temptation. It is like leprosy that has mingled itself with the wall: the wall itself must be pulled down, or the leprosy will not be cured. It is like gangrene, which mixes poison with the blood and spirits and cannot be separated from the place where it is; both must be cut off together. For instance, in David’s temptation to uncleanness, ten thousand considerations might have been brought in to stop the mouth of the temptation. But it had united itself with his lust, and nothing but killing that lust could destroy the temptation or win him the victory. This deceives many people. They have some pressing temptation that has gained some advantages and is urgent on them. They pray against it and oppose it with all kinds of powerful considerations, each of which seems enough to conquer and destroy it, or at least to overpower it so that it will never be troublesome again. Yet no good is done and no ground is gained; indeed, it grows on them more and more. What is the reason? It has incorporated and united itself with the lust, and is safe from all the opposition they make. If they would really do the work, they must set upon the whole of the lust itself: their ambition, pride, worldliness, sensuality, or whatever it is that the temptation is united with. All other dealings with it are like tampering with a spreading gangrene. The part or the whole may be kept for a little while, in great torment; but cutting off or death must come at last. With such a course the soul may torture itself for a season, but it must come to this: its lust must die, or the soul must die.

2dly. In whatever part of the soul the lust is seated that the temptation is united with, it draws #emph[the whole soul] after it by one means or another, and so forestalls or heads off any opposition. Suppose it is a lust of the mind, for there are lusts of the mind and uncleanness of the spirit, such as ambition, vainglory, and the like. What a world of ways the understanding then has to restrain the affections from clinging to God so tenaciously, since there is so much in what it aims at to give them contentment and satisfaction! It will not only forestall all the reasonings of the mind, which it necessarily does, being like a bloodshot infirmity in the eyes that presents all things to the senses and perception in its own hue and color. It will also draw the whole soul into the same frame on other accounts and side considerations. It promises the whole soul a share in the spoil it aims at, as the money Judas first desired out of covetousness was to be shared among all his lusts. Or suppose it is in the more sensual part and first takes possession of the affections. What prejudices they will bring on the understanding, how they will bribe it into consenting, what arguments and hopes they will supply it with, cannot easily be expressed, as was shown before. In short, there is no particular temptation that, when it is in its hour, does not have such a supply of help from things good, evil, and indifferent. It is fed by so many considerations that seem utterly alien and foreign to it, and in some cases it has such plausible pleas and pretexts, that its strength will easily be acknowledged.

(4.) Consider the end of any temptation. It is Satan’s end and sin’s end: that is, the dishonor of God and the ruin of our souls.

(5.) Consider what the #emph[outcome] has been of any former temptations you have had. Have they not defiled your conscience, disquieted your peace, weakened you in your obedience, and clouded the face of God? Though you were not won over to the outward evil or the full outcome of your temptation, yet have you not been defeated? Has not your soul been soiled and grievously perplexed by it? Indeed, did you ever in your life come off fairly, without some loss you could feel, from almost any temptation you have had to deal with? And would you willingly be entangled again? If you are at liberty, beware. Enter no more, if possible, lest something worse happen to you.

These, I say, are some of the many considerations that might be dwelt on to show the importance of the truth set forth, and how fully we are concerned to take care that we “will not enter into temptation.”

==== Objections Answered

Against what has been said, some objections secretly creep into men’s souls and have the power to make them negligent and careless in this matter, which is of such importance to them, a duty so absolutely necessary for those who intend to walk with God in any peace or with any faithfulness. These objections are to be considered and removed. They are the following:

#emph[Obj.] 1\. “Why should we so fear temptation and labor to avoid it? We are commanded to ‘consider it pure joy … when you#super[+] fall into temptations of many kinds’ (Jas 1:2). Now, surely I need not anxiously avoid falling into something which, when I have fallen into it, I am to count as all joy.” To this I answer:

1\. You will not hold to this rule in all things, namely, that a man need not seek to avoid something that it is his duty to rejoice in when he cannot help falling into it. The same apostle tells the rich man to “exult in his low position” (1:10). And without doubt, to the man who knows the goodness, wisdom, and love of God in his dealings, in every condition that is needful for him, it will be a matter of rejoicing. And yet how few rich, godly men can you persuade not to take care and use all lawful means so that they are not made poor and low! And in most cases, the truth is, it would be their sin not to do so. It is our business to hold our ground and to secure ourselves as well as we can; if God changes our condition, we are to rejoice in it. If the temptations mentioned here befall us, we may have cause to rejoice; but not if we fall into them by neglecting our duty.

2\. Temptations are taken in #emph[two ways]:

(1.) Passively and merely #emph[materially], for things that are, or in some cases may be, temptations; or:

(2.) #emph[Actively], for things that do entice to sin. James speaks of temptations in the first sense only. For having said, “Consider it pure joy … when you#super[+] fall into temptations of many kinds” (verse 2), he adds in verse 12, “Blessed is the man who perseveres under temptation, because when he has stood the test, he will receive the crown of life.” But a man might say, “If this is so, then temptations are good, and from God.” “No,” says James. “Take temptation in the sense of something that entices and leads to sin, and in that sense God tempts no one; but each one is tempted by his own lust” (verses 13, 14). As if he said: “To have such temptations, to be tempted to sin, is not the blessed thing I mean. What is blessed is the enduring of afflictions God sends for the trial of our faith. So, though I must count it all joy when, by the will of God, I fall into various afflictions for my trial, which still have the matter of temptation in them, yet I am to use all care and diligence so that my lust is given no occasions or advantages to tempt me to sin.”

#emph[Obj.] 2. “But was not our Savior Christ himself tempted? And is it evil to be brought into the same state and condition as he was? Indeed, it is not only said that he was tempted, but his being tempted is spoken of as something advantageous, which helps make him merciful as our high priest: ‘Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted’ (Heb 2:17, 18). And he makes it the ground of a great promise to his disciples that they had ‘stood by Me in My temptations’ (Luke 22:28).”

#emph[Ans.] It is true that our Savior was tempted. Yet his temptations are counted among the #emph[evils] that befell him in the days of his flesh, things that came on him through the malice of the world and of its prince. He did not willfully throw himself into temptation; he said that would be to “put the Lord your God to the test” (Matt 4:7). And indeed, to enter into any temptation willingly is to tempt God, to put him to the test, in a high degree. Now, our condition is such that, however much diligence and watchfulness we use, we are sure to be tempted, and to be made like Christ in that. This does not change the fact that it is our duty to do our utmost to keep from falling into temptations, and that for this reason: Christ had only the #emph[suffering] part of temptation when he entered into it; we have the #emph[sinning] part of it as well. When the prince of this world came to Christ, he had “nothing in” him; but when he comes to us, he has a part in us. So in one effect of temptations, namely trials and disquiet, we are made like Christ, and so we are to rejoice as far as that is produced by any means. Yet by another effect, our being defiled and entangled, we are made unlike him; and so we are to seek by all means to avoid them. We never come off as Christ did. Who of us “enters into temptation” and is not defiled?

#emph[Obj.] 3. “But what need is there for this great effort and carefulness? Is it not said that ‘God is faithful; He will not let you#super[+] be tempted beyond what you#super[+] can bear. But when you#super[+] are tempted, He will also provide an escape’ (1 Cor 10:13)? And ‘the Lord knows how to rescue the godly from temptations’ (2 Pet 2:9). Why, then, do we need to be anxious not to enter into them?”

#emph[Ans.] I very much question what help a man will have from God in his temptation if he willingly enters into it because he supposes God has promised to deliver him out of it. The Lord knows that, through the craft of Satan, the subtlety and malice of the world, and the deceitfulness of the sin that so easily besets us, we will enter into various temptations when we have done our utmost. In his love, care, tenderness, and faithfulness, he has provided such a sufficiency of grace for us that they will not wholly prevail to make an everlasting separation between him and our souls. Yet I have three things to say to this objection:

(1.) The man who #emph[willfully] or negligently enters into temptation has no reason in the world to promise himself any help from God, or any deliverance from the temptation he has entered into. The promise is made to those whom temptations befall in their way, whether they will or not; not to those who willfully fall into them, who run out of their way to meet them. And so the devil (as is commonly observed), when he tempted our Savior, left out the words “in all your ways” from the text of Scripture he twisted to his purpose. The promise of deliverance is to those who are in their ways; and one principal way is to beware of temptation.

(2.) Though there is a sufficiency of grace provided for all the #emph[elect], so that they will not fall utterly from God through any temptation, yet it would make any gracious heart tremble to think what dishonor to God, what scandal to the gospel, what woeful darkness and disquiet they may bring on their own souls, though they do not perish. And those who are scared by nothing but fear of hell, on whom other considerations short of that have no influence, have more reason to fear it, in my judgment, than perhaps they realize.

(3.) To enter into temptation on this account is to venture on sin “so that grace may increase” (Rom 6:1, 2), which is the same thing as to “continue in sin”; and the apostle rejects the very thought of that with the greatest abhorrence. Is it not madness for a man willingly to let the ship he is in break on a rock, with the irrecoverable loss of his merchandise, because he supposes that he himself will swim safely to shore on a plank? Is it any less madness in a man who will risk the shipwreck of all his comfort, peace, and joy, and of as much of the glory of God and the honor of the gospel as he is entrusted with, merely on the supposition that his soul will still escape? A man would think these things did not deserve to be mentioned; and yet poor souls at times deceive themselves with things like these.
